      "id": 60,
      "text": "A cryptographic math puzzle. So, it's basically a giant global lottery that happens, what, roughly"
    },
    {
      "id": 61,
      "text": "every 10 minutes, right? Yep, about every 10 minutes. All these humming supercomputers are racing to guess a winning number. Exactly."
    },
    {
      "id": 62,
      "text": "And the winner of that 10-minute lottery gets the privilege of bundling up the latest batch of user transactions and permanently adding"
    },
    {
      "id": 63,
      "text": "them to the official record, the blockchain. You've got it. And as a reward for doing this work, the winning miner receives brand"
    },
    {
      "id": 64,
      "text": "new bitcoins. Plus the transaction fees, right? Right. Plus the transaction fees from all the payments they just bundled. OK, but why"
    },
    {
      "id": 65,
      "text": "does solving a random math puzzle make it secure? Well, the genius of this design is that the work isn't just arbitrary"
    },
    {
      "id": 66,
      "text": "math. The puzzle the miners are solving is mathematically tied to the data of the previous block of"
    },
    {
      "id": 67,
      "text": "Oh, so they're chained together? Exactly. So if a malicious actor wanted to go back and alter a transaction, say to"
    },
    {
      "id": 68,
      "text": "reverse a payment they made to someone else, they wouldn't just have to solve the puzzle for that specific block. What would they have to do?"
    },
    {
      "id": 69,
      "text": "They would have to redo the computational work for that block and every single block that came after it. Wow. All while the rest"
    },
    {
      "id": 70,
      "text": "of the network is moving forward. Yes. All while racing against the combined computing power of the rest of the honest network."
    },
    {
      "id": 71,
      "text": "So what does this all mean? I mean, practically."
    },
    {
      "id": 72,
      "text": "Practically speaking? Well, it effectively replaces the human trust we put in a bank clerk with mathematical certainty. To cheat"
    },
    {
      "id": 73,
      "text": "the system, you would need more computing power than the rest of the globe combined. Which is economically and physically impractical. Practically"
    },
    {
      "id": 74,
      "text": "impossible. Yeah. But wait, what happens if a ton of new computers join the network? Like if I have a million supercomputers jump"
    },
    {
      "id": 75,
      "text": "online tomorrow to mine, wouldn't they start solving these 10-minute lotteries in, I don't know, three seconds? You would think so. But"
    },
    {
      "id": 76,
      "text": "the protocol has a brilliant self-regulating mechanism called dynamic difficulty. Dynamic difficulty. Okay."
    },
    {
      "id": 77,
      "text": "Every 2016 blocks, which takes about two weeks, the network automatically evaluates how fast the blocks"
    },
    {
      "id": 78,
      "text": "were solved. Oh, it checks itself? Right. And if they were solved too quickly because more computing power joined, the protocol literally"
    },
    {
      "id": 79,
      "text": "makes the math puzzle harder. And if computers leave? It makes the puzzle easier. It automatically maintains that 10-minute"
    }
  ]
}
